the intersection of Jabotinsky Street and Shderot HaTzionut in Petah Tikva. It serves the Nachalat Yehuda neighborhood and surrounding residential areas, with a daily passenger capacity of up to 45,000. The station's design includes three main entrances and a large platform, supporting efficient commuter flow. Its strategic position as the adjacent station to Station 35, connected by a 1.2-kilometer tunnel, further enhances its importance in the metro network. The station's construction and ongoing maintenance by Shapir Engineering, under the oversight of Yossi Cohen, ensure its operational reliability and safety [Data: Entities (1, 14, 36, 39); Relationships (49, 59, 60, 82)].\n\n## Shared critical infrastructure increases operational interdependence and risk.\n\nStation 36 and Station 35 share key infrastructure components, most notably Ventilation System A and the Fire Suppression System. This interconnectedness means that failures or incidents affecting one station can have direct consequences for the other. For example, the Fire Suppression System is a unified network that provides comprehensive fire protection across both stations, enhancing overall safety but also creating a single point of vulnerability. The shared ventilation system is essential for maintaining air quality and safety, especially given the stations' underground nature and high passenger volumes [Data: Entities (1, 32); Relationships (55, 58)].\n\n## Ventilation System A is a single point of failure with significant safety implications.\n\nVentilation System A is the primary system responsible for air quality and emergency ventilation at both Station 36 and Station 35. Its operational status is vital for passenger safety, especially during emergencies such as fires or system malfunctions. The system's failure, as occurred on March 15, 2026, necessitated the evacuation of 4,100 passengers and a temporary station closure, underscoring the critical nature of this infrastructure. The incident highlighted the need for robust maintenance and contingency planning to mitigate the risks associated with shared systems [Data: Entities (1, 21, 35); Relationships (47, 58)].\n\n## Single Fan Failure in Ventilation System A reduces capacity and disrupts service.\n\nA Single Fan Failure in Ventilation System A results in a 25% reduction in ventilation capacity, affecting both Station 36 and Station 35. This failure mode leads to reduced train frequency until repairs are completed, directly impacting commuter experience and operational efficiency. The interdependence of the stations on this system means that even partial failures can have cascading effects on metro service reliability and passenger safety [Data: Entities (21); Relationships (47)].\n\n## The 2026 ventilation failure incident demonstrates real-world impact and emergency response.\n\nOn March 15, 2026, Station 36 experienced a significant ventilation failure involving Ventilation System A, which required the evacuation of thousands of passengers and a temporary shutdown of the station. This event demonstrated the real-world consequences of infrastructure failures in high-capacity transit environments and the importance of coordinated emergency response protocols. The incident also emphasized the operational risks associated with shared systems and the need for ongoing investment in maintenance and system upgrades [Data: Entities (1, 35); Relationships (58)].\n\n## Robust fire safety is achieved through a shared Fire Suppression System.\n\nThe Fire Suppression System at Station 36 is interconnected with Station 35's system, forming a unified network that delivers reliable fire protection across both locations. This setup not only safeguards each station individually but also strengthens the overall fire safety infrastructure for the area. The shared system ensures coordinated emergency response and enhances the resilience of the metro network against fire-related incidents [Data: Entities (32); Relationships (55)].\n\n## Station 36’s construction and maintenance reflect strong technical and legal compliance.\n\nStation 36 was constructed during the 2019-2024 period by Shapir Engineering, a reputable firm responsible for both the building and ongoing maintenance of the station. Oversight by Yossi Cohen ensures adherence to safety standards and operational protocols. The station’s modern design, integration with critical safety systems, and compliance with construction timelines reflect a high level of technical and legal diligence, contributing to its reputation as a secure and reliable transit hub [Data: Entities (1, 46); Relationships (82)].\n\n## Station 36’s identity is closely tied to its urban context and accessibility.\n\nStation 36 derives its name from Shderot HaTzionut, the prominent street at its location, and is situated at a major intersection with Jabotinsky Street. This naming convention provides a clear reference point for passengers and connects the metro system to the surrounding urban environment. The station’s three main entrances (North, South, and Central Plaza) enhance accessibility for commuters from the Nachalat Yehuda neighborhood and beyond, reinforcing its role as a key node in the city’s transportation network [Data: Entities (1, 14, 39, 36); Relationships (49, 59, 60)].", "# Station 36 Unavailability Incident and Alternative Routing via Station 37\n\nThis community centers on the operational dynamics and contingency planning associated with the potential unavailability of Station 36. The key entities are Station 36, Station 37 (Central Hub), and the incident scenario labeled 'Station 36 Unavailability.' The relationships detail how, in the event of Station 36 becoming inaccessible, passengers are redirected to Station 37, and emergency evacuation protocols are activated. The structure highlights the importance of alternative routing and emergency preparedness within the transit network.\n\n## Station 36's critical role in network continuity\n\nStation 36 is a pivotal node within the transit network, and its unavailability triggers a cascade of operational changes. The incident scenario specifically addresses the consequences of Station 36 being out of service, which includes rerouting passengers and potentially having trains bypass the station entirely. This underscores the station's importance in maintaining regular service and the potential disruption its loss could cause to both daily operations and emergency response [Data: Entities (34); Relationships (57)].\n\n## Station 37 as the designated alternative during disruptions\n\nStation 37, also known as the Central Hub, is identified as the primary alternative for passengers when Station 36 is unavailable. Located just 0.8 km away via tunnel, Station 37's proximity and capacity make it a logical fallback point. The relationship data explicitly states that passengers are directed to use Station 37 in such scenarios, highlighting the importance of clear communication and logistical planning to ensure smooth transitions during incidents [Data: Entities (31); Relationships (64)].\n\n## Activation of emergency evacuation routes\n\nThe incident scenario includes the activation of emergency evacuation routes, indicating a comprehensive approach to passenger safety. This measure is crucial in situations where Station 36's unavailability may be due to emergencies such as fire, security threats, or infrastructure failure. The explicit mention of evacuation protocols demonstrates a commitment to risk mitigation and preparedness for high-impact events [Data: Entities (34)].\n\n## Operational consequences: trains may bypass Station 36\n\nOne of the operational responses to Station 36's unavailability is that trains may pass through the station without stopping. This adjustment is necessary to maintain schedule integrity and minimize broader network disruption. However, it also means that passengers who would normally board or alight at Station 36 must be adequately informed and provided with alternative options, such as using Station 35 or Station 37 [Data: Entities (34)].\n\n## Interconnectedness of incident response and station infrastructure\n\nThe relationships between Station 36, Station 37, and the incident scenario illustrate the interconnected nature of transit infrastructure and emergency response. The ability to redirect passengers and activate evacuation routes depends on the physical proximity of alternative stations and the robustness of contingency planning. This interconnectedness is vital for minimizing service disruption and ensuring passenger safety during incidents [Data: Entities (31, 34); Relationships (57, 64)].\n\n## Potential for passenger confusion and need for effective communication\n\nRedirecting passengers from Station 36 to Station 37, especially during emergencies, presents a risk of confusion and crowding. Effective communication strategies, including signage, announcements, and staff guidance, are essential to ensure that passengers are aware of the changes and can navigate the network safely. The incident scenario's emphasis on alternative routing and evacuation highlights the need for clear, timely information dissemination [Data: Entities (34, 31); Relationships (64)].\n\n## Implications for legal compliance and safety standards\n\nThe activation of emergency evacuation routes and the redirection of passengers to alternative stations are measures that align with legal and regulatory requirements for public safety in transit systems. Ensuring that these protocols are up-to-date and regularly tested is critical for compliance and for minimizing liability in the event of an incident. The scenario described reflects adherence to such standards [Data: Entities (34); Relationships (57, 64)].", "# Shapir Engineering and Yossi Cohen – M1 Metro Line Station Construction\n\nThis community centers on Shapir Engineering, a major construction contractor, and its project manager Yossi Cohen, who together are responsible for the development and construction of Station 35 and Station 36 on the M1 Metro Line. Shapir Engineering oversees all aspects of construction, including tunneling and emergency procedures, while Yossi Cohen manages the execution and coordination of these projects. The relationships within this community highlight a tightly integrated structure, with Yossi Cohen acting as the key representative and operational leader for Shapir Engineering at both stations. The significance of these infrastructure projects, their technical complexity, and the leadership roles involved contribute to the community's overall impact.\n\n## Shapir Engineering is the primary contractor for Station 35 and Station 36 on the M1 Metro Line.\n\nShapir Engineering is responsible for the construction of both Station 35 and Station 36, two key stations on the M1 Metro Line. Their scope of work includes not only the physical construction of the stations but also the use of advanced tunneling methods, such as the cut-and-cover technique and Tunnel Boring Machine (TBM) operations for Station 36. The company’s responsibilities extend to managing emergency procedures and station management, indicating a comprehensive role in the project lifecycle. The construction period spans from 2019 to 2024, underscoring the long-term commitment and scale of the undertaking [Data: Entities (4)].\n\n## Yossi Cohen serves as the project manager and key representative for Shapir Engineering at both stations.\n\nYossi Cohen is the designated project manager from Shapir Engineering, overseeing the construction of both Station 35 and Station 36. His role is pivotal, encompassing the management of construction processes, coordination of project activities, and ensuring adherence to required standards and timelines. As the representative of Shapir Engineering, he is responsible for maintaining communication with stakeholders and addressing challenges that arise during the project. His leadership is critical to the successful execution of the company’s objectives at these locations [Data: Entities (5); Relationships (3, 19)].\n\n## The relationship between Shapir Engineering and Yossi Cohen is highly integrated and operationally significant.\n\nThe data highlights a close operational relationship between Shapir Engineering and Yossi Cohen, with the latter acting as both the project manager and the company’s representative for Station 35 and Station 36. This integration ensures that project oversight, coordination, and representation of company interests are streamlined, reducing the risk of miscommunication and enhancing project efficiency. The dual role of Yossi Cohen strengthens accountability and provides a single point of contact for all matters related to the stations’ construction [Data: Relationships (19)].\n\n## Technical capabilities include advanced tunneling and construction methods.\n\nShapir Engineering demonstrates significant technical capabilities through its use of both cut-and-cover and Tunnel Boring Machine (TBM) methods, particularly for Station 36. These methods are essential for large-scale urban infrastructure projects, allowing for efficient and safe construction in complex environments. The company’s responsibility for tunneling work and emergency procedures further indicates a high level of technical expertise and operational readiness, which are critical for the successful delivery of metro infrastructure [Data: Entities (4)].\n\n## Project management and coordination are centralized under Yossi Cohen’s leadership.\n\nYossi Cohen’s role as project manager involves coordinating various teams and resources, addressing challenges, and ensuring that construction meets required specifications and standards. His leadership is described as critical to the successful completion of Station 35 and Station 36, suggesting that project management is highly centralized. This centralization can lead to more effective decision-making and streamlined project execution, but also places significant responsibility on a single individual [Data: Entities (5); Relationships (3)].\n\n## The construction period (2019–2024) reflects a long-term, high-impact infrastructure project.\n\nThe construction of Station 35 and Station 36 is scheduled over a five-year period, from 2019 to 2024. This extended timeline is typical for major infrastructure projects and reflects the complexity and scale of the work involved. The duration also implies sustained engagement from Shapir Engineering and Yossi Cohen, as well as ongoing coordination with stakeholders and authorities throughout the project lifecycle [Data: Entities (4)].\n\n## Shapir Engineering’s responsibilities extend beyond construction to include emergency procedures and station management.\n\nIn addition to building the stations, Shapir Engineering is tasked with overseeing emergency procedures and station management for both Station 35 and Station 36. This broader scope of responsibility indicates that the company is not only focused on construction but also on operational readiness and safety, which are essential for public infrastructure projects. Such responsibilities require robust planning, training, and coordination with emergency services [Data: Entities (4)].\n\n## The community’s structure is characterized by clear roles and direct lines of accountability.\n\nThe data reveals a community structure with well-defined roles: Shapir Engineering as the contractor and Yossi Cohen as the project manager and representative. This clarity in roles and direct lines of accountability facilitate effective project governance and risk management. The explicit assignment of responsibilities helps ensure that project objectives are met and that any issues can be promptly addressed [Data: Entities (4, 5); Relationships (3, 19)].", "# Station 36 (Shderot HaTzionut) and Shared Metro Infrastructure\n\nThis community centers on Station 36 (Shderot HaTzionut), a major underground metro station in Petah Tikva, Israel, and its interconnected infrastructure, including Ventilation System A, the Fire Suppression System, and adjacent stations. Station 36 is a critical transit hub serving the Nachalat Yehuda neighborhood, with a daily capacity of up to 45,000 passengers. Its operational integrity is closely tied to shared systems with Station 35, notably Ventilation System A and the Fire Suppression System, making it vulnerable to cascading failures. The community's structure is defined by these interdependencies, the history of construction and maintenance by Shapir Engineering, and the presence of contingency plans for incidents such as ventilation failures and station unavailability. The technical sophistication and scale of Station 36, combined with its essential role in the metro network, underscore the high impact of any disruptions within this community.\n\n## Station 36 is a critical transit hub with high passenger throughput.\n\nStation 36 (Shderot HaTzionut) is a deep underground metro station strategically located at the intersection of Jabotinsky Street and Shderot HaTzionut, serving the Nachalat Yehuda neighborhood and surrounding areas. With a platform 150 meters long and 12 meters wide, it is designed to accommodate up to 45,000 passengers daily, making it a major node in the Israel M1 Metro Line. The station features three main entrances (North, South, and Central Plaza) to facilitate efficient passenger flow. Its significance is further highlighted by its adjacency to Station 35 and Station 37, ensuring connectivity and redundancy within the metro network. The station's construction and ongoing maintenance by Shapir Engineering, under the oversight of Yossi Cohen, reinforce its importance in the city's public transportation infrastructure [Data: Entities (1, 14, 36, 31, 39); Relationships (59, 60, 8, 55, 57, 58, 64)].\n\n## Shared infrastructure with Station 35 creates operational interdependence and risk.\n\nStation 36 shares critical infrastructure with Station 35, most notably Ventilation System A and the Fire Suppression System. This interconnection means that failures in these systems can simultaneously impact both stations, increasing the risk of widespread service disruptions. For example, Ventilation System A is essential for maintaining air quality and safety in both stations, and any malfunction—such as a single fan failure—reduces system capacity and necessitates reduced train frequency until repairs are made. The Fire Suppression System is also jointly operated, providing comprehensive fire protection across both facilities. This shared approach enhances safety but also introduces the potential for cascading failures, where an incident in one station can compromise the safety and operation of the other [Data: Entities (1, 32, 21, 35); Relationships (8, 34, 55, 58, 26)].\n\n## Ventilation System A is a single point of failure with significant operational consequences.\n\nVentilation System A is a critical, shared system serving both Station 36 and Station 35. Its proper functioning is vital for passenger safety and station operability. A failure in this system, such as the incident on March 15, 2026, led to the evacuation of 4,100 passengers and a temporary station closure, underscoring the system's importance. The 'Single Fan Failure' mode reduces the system's capacity to 75%, directly impacting train frequency and passenger comfort. The dependency of both stations on this single system makes it a high-risk point in the network, necessitating robust maintenance and contingency planning [Data: Entities (1, 21, 35); Relationships (8, 34, 58, 26)].\n\n## Incident response protocols are in place for station unavailability and system failures.\n\nThe community has established incident response protocols for scenarios such as Station 36 unavailability and Ventilation System A failure. In the event of Station 36 becoming unavailable, passengers are directed to use adjacent stations (Station 35 or Station 37), and emergency evacuation routes are activated. Similarly, a failure in Ventilation System A triggers immediate operational adjustments, including possible station closures and reduced train frequency. These protocols are designed to minimize disruption and ensure passenger safety, reflecting a high level of preparedness for technical failures and emergencies [Data: Entities (34, 35, 31); Relationships (57, 58, 64)].\n\n## Robust fire safety is achieved through an integrated suppression system.\n\nFire safety at Station 36 is ensured by a comprehensive Fire Suppression System that is interconnected with Station 35's system. This unified network provides reliable fire suppression capabilities across both stations, enhancing emergency response and overall safety. The shared system not only safeguards each station individually but also strengthens the fire safety infrastructure for the entire area served by these stations. This approach demonstrates a commitment to high safety standards and operational resilience [Data: Entities (32, 1, 35); Relationships (55)].\n\n## The 2019-2024 construction period established a modern, resilient infrastructure.\n\nStation 36 and Station 35 were constructed during the 2019-2024 period by Shapir Engineering, resulting in modern facilities equipped with advanced safety and operational systems. The construction phase laid the foundation for the current interdependent infrastructure, including shared ventilation and fire suppression systems. The involvement of a reputable engineering firm and dedicated oversight by Yossi Cohen contribute to the stations' ongoing reliability and safety. This modern infrastructure supports high passenger volumes and complex operational requirements [Data: Entities (1, 46, 35); Relationships (78)].\n\n## Station 36's location integrates urban and transit networks.\n\nStation 36 is situated at a key urban intersection (Jabotinsky Street and Shderot HaTzionut), directly connecting the metro system to the surrounding Nachalat Yehuda neighborhood. This integration enhances accessibility for residents and supports the broader urban transit network. The station's naming and design reflect its role as a reference point within the city, facilitating wayfinding and urban connectivity. Its proximity to major streets and neighborhoods further amplifies its importance as a transit hub [Data: Entities (1, 14, 36, 39); Relationships (59, 60, 26)].\n\n## Contingency planning ensures network resilience during disruptions.\n\nIn the event of Station 36's unavailability, contingency plans direct passengers to use Station 35 or Station 37 as alternatives, and operational adjustments such as trains passing through without stopping are implemented. These measures are supported by emergency evacuation routes and communication protocols, ensuring that disruptions are managed efficiently and safely. The presence of adjacent stations within close proximity (1.2 km to Station 35 and 0.8 km to Station 37) provides redundancy and maintains service continuity during incidents [Data: Entities (34, 31, 1); Relationships (57, 64)].", "# Station 35 (Kiryat Arie) and Critical Metro Infrastructure\n\nThis community centers on Station 35 (Kiryat Arie), a major underground metro station in Petah Tikva, Israel, which serves as the northernmost point of the initial M1 Metro Line segment. The station is a critical transit hub, supporting tens of thousands of daily passengers and featuring advanced infrastructure systems, including shared ventilation and fire suppression with the adjacent Station 36. The community's structure is defined by the interdependence of Station 35 with key technical systems such as Ventilation System A, Transformer 35A, and air quality sensors, as well as its integration into the broader metro network. Notably, the community has experienced a significant infrastructure failure involving the ventilation system, highlighting both operational vulnerabilities and the importance of robust safety protocols. The relationships among these entities underscore the potential for high-impact incidents affecting passenger safety, operational continuity, and regional mobility.\n\n## Station 35 is a critical transit hub with high passenger throughput.\n\nStation 35, also known as Kiryat Arie, is a major underground metro station strategically located in the Kiryat Arie business district of Petah Tikva. It is designed to accommodate up to 35,000 daily passengers and 6,500 during peak hours, making it a vital node in the Israel M1 Metro Line. The station's infrastructure includes two main entrances—one serving the business district and the other the residential area—ensuring accessibility for a diverse commuter base. Its position as the northernmost station on the initial M1 segment further amplifies its importance for regional connectivity and economic activity [Data: Entities (0, 13, 44, 42, 43); Relationships (76, 74, 75)].\n\n## Shared critical infrastructure increases operational risk.\n\nStation 35 shares several essential systems with the adjacent Station 36, most notably Ventilation System A and the fire suppression system. This interdependence means that a failure in one system can simultaneously compromise both stations, as evidenced by the March 15, 2026 incident where a ventilation failure necessitated the evacuation of thousands of passengers and a temporary station closure. The shared nature of these systems underscores the need for coordinated maintenance and rapid response protocols to mitigate cascading failures and ensure passenger safety [Data: Entities (0, 41); Relationships (1, 73)].\n\n## Ventilation System A is a single point of failure for both Station 35 and Station 36.\n\nVentilation System A is the primary system responsible for maintaining air quality and safety at both Station 35 and Station 36. Its failure can result in the loss of ventilation for both stations, potentially affecting up to 80,000 daily passengers and requiring large-scale evacuations. The March 2026 incident highlighted the vulnerability of relying on a single system for multiple critical locations, emphasizing the importance of redundancy and robust monitoring [Data: Entities (41, 10); Relationships (1, 73, 5)].\n\n## Air quality sensors play a vital role in early incident detection.\n\nAir quality sensors installed at Station 35 and Station 36 are instrumental in detecting reduced airflow and other anomalies associated with ventilation system failures. During the 2026 incident, these sensors provided immediate feedback on the compromised air quality, enabling a timely evacuation and minimizing potential health risks to passengers. Their integration into the station's safety protocols demonstrates the value of real-time monitoring in managing critical infrastructure [Data: Entities (10); Relationships (5)].\n\n## Station 35’s power supply is secured via Transformer 35A and the Central Power Grid.\n\nStation 35 is connected to the Central Power Grid through Transformer 35A, ensuring a stable and reliable power supply for its operations. This connection is essential for maintaining the functionality of all station systems, including lighting, signaling, ventilation, and safety mechanisms. Any disruption to this power supply could have significant operational and safety implications, further highlighting the station's reliance on robust infrastructure [Data: Entities (40); Relationships (70)].\n\n## The cut-and-cover construction method shapes the station’s design and risk profile.\n\nBoth Station 35 and Station 36 were constructed using the cut-and-cover method, which involves excavating a trench, constructing the station, and then covering it. This method influences the stations' structural characteristics, emergency access, and maintenance requirements. While it allows for efficient construction in urban environments, it also presents unique challenges in terms of waterproofing, ventilation, and long-term durability, all of which are relevant to the station's ongoing operational risk assessment [Data: Entities (45); Relationships (77)].\n\n## Operational oversight and maintenance are managed by Shapir Engineering and Yossi Cohen.\n\nThe maintenance and operational integrity of Station 35 are overseen by Shapir Engineering, with Yossi Cohen designated as the responsible person. This management structure is critical for ensuring compliance with safety regulations, timely maintenance, and effective incident response. The presence of a clearly identified responsible party supports accountability and facilitates communication during both routine operations and emergencies [Data: Entities (0)].\n\n## The station’s dual-entrance design enhances accessibility and evacuation options.\n\nStation 35 features two main entrances: the Business District Entrance and the Residential Entrance. This dual-entrance design not only improves accessibility for commuters from different areas but also provides multiple evacuation routes in the event of an emergency. The availability of alternative exits is a key factor in the station's emergency preparedness and passenger safety strategy [Data: Entities (42, 43); Relationships (74, 75)].\n\n## A major infrastructure failure has already occurred, demonstrating real-world risk.\n\nOn March 15, 2026, a failure in Ventilation System A led to the evacuation of 3,200 passengers and a temporary closure of Station 35. This incident serves as a concrete example of the operational risks inherent in the station's design and interdependencies. It underscores the need for ongoing investment in system reliability, emergency planning, and rapid incident response to protect public safety and maintain service continuity [Data: Entities (0, 41); Relationships (1, 73, 5)].", "# Israel M1 Metro Line: Ventilation System A and Critical Infrastructure Community\n\nThis community centers on Ventilation System A, a vital shared infrastructure system serving Station 35 and Station 36 of the Israel M1 Metro Line. The system's operation is supported by a network of technical subsystems, including power supply, SCADA control, fire detection, and redundancy features, all maintained by Climatec Systems Ltd. The reliability of Ventilation System A is crucial for the safety and comfort of approximately 80,000 daily passengers, as evidenced by a significant incident in March 2026 that necessitated station evacuations. The community's structure is defined by tightly integrated technical, operational, and maintenance relationships, with a strong emphasis on regular inspection, emergency preparedness, and contractor accountability.\n\n## Ventilation System A is a critical safety and operational asset for the Israel M1 Metro Line.\n\nVentilation System A provides essential air circulation, smoke extraction, and temperature control for both Station 35 and Station 36, directly affecting the safety and comfort of approximately 80,000 daily passengers. Its failure can result in immediate evacuation and loss of operational capacity, as demonstrated by the March 15, 2026 incident caused by a degraded power supply unit. The system's centrality to passenger safety and metro operations underscores its high criticality within the community [Data: Entities (2, 9, 15); Relationships (17, 27)].\n\n## The system's technical architecture incorporates redundancy, automation, and emergency response integration.\n\nVentilation System A is composed of main and backup fan units, air shafts, and an automated control system with manual override. Redundancy is provided by two 250 kW backup fans, ensuring continued operation in the event of main fan failure. The SCADA Control System enables remote monitoring and control, while the Fire Detection System can trigger emergency smoke extraction and automatically notify the Fire Department. This layered technical design enhances resilience and rapid response capabilities during emergencies [Data: Entities (2, 17, 18, 23, 24, 25, 30); Relationships (29, 30, 33, 36, 37, 38, 39)].\n\n## Maintenance and operational reliability are managed by Climatec Systems Ltd. through a structured program.\n\nClimatec Systems Ltd. is responsible for the ongoing maintenance, annual complete overhaul, and 24/7 emergency support of Ventilation System A. The maintenance program includes weekly filter inspection and cleaning, monthly fan motor inspections, and quarterly full system tests (including emergency mode). This structured approach is designed to ensure system reliability and compliance with safety standards, reducing the risk of unplanned outages [Data: Entities (3, 19, 26, 27, 28, 29); Relationships (15, 40, 41, 42, 43, 51)].\n\n## A single point of failure in the power supply unit can have cascading effects on metro operations.\n\nThe main power supply unit is a critical component whose failure, as seen in March 2026 due to capacitor degradation, led to the complete shutdown of Ventilation System A and the evacuation of both stations it serves. This incident highlights the importance of component-level reliability and the need for robust preventive maintenance and rapid response protocols to mitigate cascading operational impacts [Data: Entities (2, 9); Relationships (17)].\n\n## The community demonstrates strong integration between technical systems and emergency services.\n\nThe Fire Detection System is directly linked to both the ventilation system's emergency smoke extraction mode and the automatic notification of the Fire Department. This integration ensures that in the event of smoke or fire, both technical and human responses are rapidly coordinated, minimizing risk to passengers and infrastructure [Data: Entities (18, 20); Relationships (30, 33)].\n\n## Regular and comprehensive maintenance events are institutionalized to uphold system integrity.\n\nThe annual 'Complete Overhaul' event, performed by Climatec Systems Ltd., involves comprehensive inspection and servicing of Ventilat